      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "We start by visualizing the problem.\nWe can create two separate triangles to help us solve the problem.\nFirst, notice that triangles $ABC$ and $BCD$ share side $BC$.\nWe can use the fact that the sum of angles in a triangle is $180^\\circ$.\nSince triangles $ABC$ and $BCD$ share side $BC$, the sum of angles $ABC$ and $BCD$ is $180^\\circ$.\nThis means that triangle $ACC$ is actually equilateral because all angles are equal.\nTherefore, $AC=AB+BC=2+5=7$ units.\nNext, we can apply the same reasoning to triangle $ABD$.\n\nTriangles $ABC$ and $DBD$ share side $BD$.\nWe can use the fact that the sum of angles in a triangle is $180^\\circ$.\nSince triangles $ABC$ and $DBD$ share side $BD$, the sum of angles $ABC$ and $CBD$ is $180^\\circ$.\nThis means that triangle $BDI$ is also equilateral because all angles are equal.\nTherefore, $BD=AB+AD=5+14=19$ units.\n\nFinally, we can use the fact that $AC$ is greater than $BD$.\nThe ratio of $AC$ to $BD$ is $\\frac{7}{9}$.\n\n$\\begin{array}{|c|c|c|c|}\\hline \\text{Point }&\\text{AB}&\\text{BC}&\\text{AC}\\\\ \\hline \\text{A}&&&&\\\\ \\hline \\text{B}&2&5&\\\\ \\hline \\text{C}&7&&&\\\\ \\hline \\text{D}~&14&&\\\\ \\hline \\end{array}$\nThe answer is: 14",
